note bottom of "ores::workflow::messaging::registrar"
Two families, two shapes. A message registrar subscribes
its own per-entity subjects and returns its own
subscriptions. A history provider registers on one shared
table instead, because a single generic subject serves
both entities, so the table has to outlive the one
subscription -- which is why it is a function-local
static rather than a local.
end note

note right of "ores::workflow::service::workflow_engine"
Every state change is persisted before the next command is
published, so a restart resumes in-flight instances from
recover_in_progress() rather than losing them. A step is
addressed by its step_id, which doubles as the idempotency
key that lets a domain service recognise a re-dispatched
command and answer with the result it already produced.

The engine resolves a workflow's type through the
workflow_registry it is handed. That type and its
workflow_definition belong to ores.workflow.api, so they are
drawn on that module's diagram rather than repeated here.
end note

note bottom of "ores::workflow::messaging::workflow_query_handler"
get_steps returns the step-log projection the shell prints.
A step can report success and still have skipped work, so
the log travels beside the status rather than inside the
error, and a reader that prints only the status cannot tell
a clean run from a lossy one.
end note

